;; The tracker envelope, pinned.
;;
;; Tracker seals one thing: the `description` body of a Recipe-less item — a
;; task, an issue, a meeting, a journal entry, a resource, a category — and only
;; for the one user whose `users.seal_prose` is 1. Titles, names and tags stay
;; clear, because they are what the search runs on and what he chose to find
;; things by. Prose was never that.
;;
;; **This file exists because three implementations must not disagree.**
;;
;;   tracker/src/cljs/et/tr/ui/seal.cljs   the browser, WebCrypto
;;   tracker/src/clj/et/tr/envelope.clj    the server, which holds no key and
;;                                         knows only the prefix and the columns
;;   plurama-cli/tracker_seal.clj          the CLI, the agents and the proxy
;;
;; A docstring claiming they agree is not a contract. An assertion against this
;; file is. Every suite reads it and refuses to pass without it — a drift control
;; you can silently not run is not one.
;;
;; ## Regenerating
;;
;; Don't, casually. Regenerating changes every ciphertext in here, which is a
;; deliberate act — a new envelope version — and never a way to make a red test
;; go green. A red test here means an implementation moved, and the implementation
;; is what should move back.
;;
;; ## The key in this file is a test key
;;
;; `:key-base64` is a throwaway, generated for this file and used nowhere. The
;; vectors are meant to be readable by anyone with the repo; that is the point of
;; them. **Never put the real key here**, and note that the real one is a
;; *different* secret from cookbook's — one envelope, two apps, two keys, because
;; a leaked key cannot be rotated out of the data it already sealed.
;;
;; ## What each key means
;;
;;   :envelope          the shape, stated so a reader need not infer it
;;   :key-fingerprint   the eight characters every surface shows — the ⚙ panel,
;;                      the proxy's startup line, the walker's header. Pinned
;;                      here because a comparison two sides compute differently
;;                      is a lie that reads as a check.
;;   :key-base64        the key every :vectors entry was sealed under
;;   :other-key-base64  a second key, used only by the wrong-key tamper case
;;   :binding           which name a table's values are bound under — the AAD's
;;                      first half. **Nine tables, one binding**, and that is the
;;                      schema being told the truth rather than sloppiness: the
;;                      server copies a body from `issues` into `tasks` when an
;;                      issue is converted, and a category keeps its id when its
;;                      Group changes. Bind per table and the first conversion
;;                      seals a value shut in the column it lands in.
;;   :sealed-columns    the inventory. Nine tables, one column each.
;;   :clear-tables      named so that a reader knows the omission is a decision.
;;                      `messages` above all: its bodies are written by three
;;                      producers that hold no key and cannot be given one — the
;;                      IMAP poller, blog's forwarding, and tracker's own feed
;;                      worker — and its list search reads the body. `mottos` is
;;                      the other judgement: a motto's description is a second
;;                      name for the same thing (Carpe Diem / Seize the day), not
;;                      prose, and the model's line is names clear, prose sealed.
;;   :event-prose       where prose hides inside `events.payload`, which is JSON
;;                      and not a column. Going forward the server seals none of
;;                      this and needs to seal none of it: it copies values that
;;                      arrived sealed. The historical rows are the walker's job,
;;                      and the browser's audit view unseals what it renders.
;;   :vectors           nonce + plaintext + the exact ciphertext it must produce.
;;                      Seal with that nonce and you must get that string, byte
;;                      for byte; unseal that string and you must get the
;;                      plaintext back.
;;   :blank             values the seal must hand back untouched — nil is the
;;                      sixth of them and cannot be written in a vector beside
;;                      strings without reading as one, so the suites add it
;;                      themselves. Never-seal-blank is what leaves
;;                      `prune-empty-entries` able to see an empty journal entry.
;;   :round-trip        seal then unseal, under a fresh nonce each time
;;   :passthrough       values unsealing must hand back untouched, because they
;;                      do not carry the prefix. The near-misses are the point.
;;   :unopenable        values that carry the prefix and will not open. This is
;;                      the one class where two hosts genuinely diverge — `AAA`
;;                      throws in Java and decodes in JavaScript — which is why
;;                      it is pinned as an *answer* rather than a route: all
;;                      three implementations must hand the value back as it is,
;;                      visibly, and none may throw.
;;   :tamper            envelopes that must fail to open. The last of them is the
;;                      cross-app case: a body sealed under cookbook's
;;                      `recipe/description` binding, offered to tracker as an
;;                      `item/description`. It must not open. Two apps sharing one
;;                      envelope and one laptop is exactly when that matters.
{:envelope
 {:prefix "enc:v1:",
  :cipher "AES-256-GCM",
  :nonce-bytes 12,
  :tag-bits 128,
  :key-bytes 32,
  :layout "enc:v1: ++ base64(nonce || ciphertext || tag)",
  :aad "utf-8 bytes of \"<binding>/<column>\", see :binding"},
 :sealed-columns
 {:journal_entries ["description"],
  :issues ["description"],
  :tasks ["description"],
  :recurring_tasks ["description"],
  :meeting_series ["description"],
  :categories ["description"],
  :meets ["description"],
  :resources ["description"],
  :journals ["description"]},
 :unopenable
